Q: How to Manipulate JSON AST in Scala A: <p>Let me also give you the <a href="http://github.com/wspringer/sonofjson" rel="nofollow noreferrer">SON of JSON</a>, which I am the developer of, version:</p>
<pre><code>import nl.typeset.sonofjson._

val json = parse(&quot;&quot;&quot;{ &quot;foo&quot; : 1, &quot;bar&quot; : { &quot;foo&quot; : 2 } }&quot;&quot;&quot;)

// or, perhaps a little easier
val json = obj(foo = 1, bar = obj(foo = 2))

json.foo = &quot;3&quot;
json.foobar = &quot;3&quot;
</code></pre>

Q: How to convert XML to JSON in node.js without having first two elements from xml file? A: <p>Warning: shameless plug:</p>
<p><a href="https://github.com/wspringer/cruftless" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Cruftless</a>, which I am the developer of, is <em>almost</em> able to do it — but not entirely. Cruftless aims to be a library that allows you to have a single definition of the structure of an XML file, and then use that to both parse data from a document or produce an XML document from the data. As a consequence, it works a bit different than all of the examples above:</p>
<pre><code>const xml = …
const template = parse(`&lt;catalog&gt;
  &lt;book id=&quot;{{id}}&quot; c-bind=&quot;books|array&quot;&gt;
    &lt;author&gt;{{author}}&lt;/author&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;{{title}}&lt;/title&gt;
    &lt;genre&gt;{{genre}}&lt;/genre&gt;
    &lt;price&gt;{{price}}&lt;/price&gt;
    &lt;publish_date&gt;{{publicationDate}}&lt;/publish_date&gt;
    &lt;description&gt;{{desc}}&lt;/description&gt;
  &lt;/book&gt;
&lt;/catalog&gt;`)
template.fromXML(xml)
</code></pre>
<p>produces:</p>
<pre><code>{
  &quot;books&quot;: [
    {
      &quot;id&quot;: &quot;bk101&quot;,
      &quot;author&quot;: &quot;Gambardella, Matthew&quot;,
      &quot;title&quot;: &quot;XML Developer's Guide&quot;,
      &quot;genre&quot;: &quot;Computer&quot;,
      &quot;price&quot;: &quot;44.95&quot;,
      &quot;publicationDate&quot;: &quot;2000-10-01&quot;,
      &quot;desc&quot;: &quot;An in-depth look at creating applications \n      with XML.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;id&quot;: &quot;bk102&quot;,
      &quot;author&quot;: &quot;Ralls, Kim&quot;,
      &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Midnight Rain&quot;,
      &quot;genre&quot;: &quot;Fantasy&quot;,
      &quot;price&quot;: &quot;5.95&quot;,
      &quot;publicationDate&quot;: &quot;2000-12-16&quot;,
      &quot;desc&quot;: &quot;A former architect battles corporate zombies, \n      an evil sorceress, and her own childhood to become queen \n      of the world.&quot;
    }
  ]
}
</code></pre>

Q: Redis SCAN not giving all matches in NodeJS A: <p>You can use <a href="https://github.com/wspringer/redis-async-gen" rel="nofollow noreferrer">redis-async-gen</a>, which I am the developer of, which prevents you from having to carefully control all of this yourself. (Read more about it <a href="https://dev.to/eastpole/redis-async-generators-3ed" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>.)</p>
<pre><code>const redis = require('redis')
const client = redis.createClient(…)
const generators = require('redis-async-gen')
const { keysMatching } = generators.using(client)

const results = []
for await (const key of keysMatching('test*')) {
  results.push(key)
}
</code></pre>

Q: Convert XML to JSON with NodeJS A: <p><a href="https://github.com/wspringer/cruftless" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Cruftless</a>, which I am the developer of, allows you to 'annotate' the data structure you want to match, to specify how it should be bound to a JSON representation of that same data.</p>
<p>So, if you define your template like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-coffee prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;TestScenario&gt;
  &lt;TestSuite name=&quot;{{name}}&quot;&gt;&lt;?bind suites|array?&gt;
    &lt;TestCaseName name=&quot;{{name}}&quot;&gt;{{data}}&lt;/TestCaseName&gt;&lt;?bind cases|array?&gt;
  &lt;/TestSuite&gt;
&lt;/TestScenario&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Then by calling <code>.fromXML</code> on the object created from it, passing in the XML you want to parse, you will get:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  suites: [
    { name: 'tc_Logout', data: 'dt_EdgeCaseRoute' },
    {
      name: 'tc_AddContract',
      data: 'dt_EdgeCaseHome,dt_EdgeCaseSpectrum'
    },
    { name: 'tc_Payment', data: 'dt_EdgeCaseRoute' },
    { name: 'tc_ClientFeedback', data: 'dt_EdgeCaseSpectrum' }
  ]
}
</code></pre>
